 ## Overview
-Optimized HAVS (Sorted k-buffer) is an optimization of the Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS) algorithm that maintains the k-buffer in a sorted state (ascending order of fragment distance) to reduce the computational cost of fragment-level visibility sorting [S1]. While standard HAVS requires $2k-1$ comparisons to identify the two nearest fragments for compositing, this method reduces that requirement to $k$ comparisons per fragment after the buffer is full [S1]. It is primarily used for simulating accurate radiographs involving attenuation and attenuation-emission cases, leveraging modern graphics hardware capabilities [S2, S2, unofficial].
+Optimized HAVS (Sorted k-buffer) is an optimization of the Hardware Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS) algorithm that maintains the k-buffer in ascending order of fragment distance to reduce the computational cost of fragment-level visibility sorting [S1]. While standard HAVS requires $2k-1$ comparisons to identify the two nearest fragments for compositing, this method reduces that requirement to $k$ comparisons per fragment after the buffer is full [S1]. It is primarily used for simulating accurate radiographs involving attenuation and attenuation-emission cases, leveraging modern graphics hardware capabilities [S2, unofficial].
 
